\name{targeting_spec}
\alias{targeting_spec}
\title{Cross-fitted finite-path and continuous-time common-law targeting}
\description{The targeting argument of coherentSurv selects a common-law targeted estimator. Its observation experiment and population target are specified explicitly by targeting_spec.}
\details{
For targeting="finite_path", supply a list with path and recorded column names, a complete-support by target feature matrix, optional baseline strata column names, subject folds, training-fitted initial_laws and recording probabilities, control, and an optional horizon label. Data have one row per independent subject. The MAR and regularity conditions of cs_target apply.

For targeting="continuous", the list requires horizon (positive analysis end time) and censor (a binary right-censor indicator column). Optional entries are strata (baseline discrete column names; default all formula variables), folds (subject labels or a column name), nfolds (default 5), grid (default 64), history_cap (from cs_control), working ("loglinear" or "pooled"), nonrecurrent (declared one-time event labels), maxit (60), tolerance (1e-7/sqrt(n)), and min_censor_survival (0.001). A subject ending before the horizon must have an explicit terminal or censoring indicator. Baseline strata must remain constant within subjects. Histories start at zero and are contiguous through right censoring or terminal absorption. Unobserved interior intervals and simultaneous distinct events are not converted to event-free exposure.

The default working event model is a stratum-specific Poisson log-linear model on retained counts, fitted separately within each training fold. The pooled option suppresses state effects in that working model. The censor nuisance is a constant stratum-specific censoring hazard. A positive mark-by-state rate_offset matrix supplies known offsets instead. Advanced callers may supply initial_rates with dimensions (K+1) by S by grid by (folds times strata), and censor_rates with one value per fold-stratum. These supplied nuisances must satisfy training-fold separation. Mark K+1 is terminal; state S+1 is absorbing. At most six recurrent marks, 128 living states, and 30 baseline strata are currently supported.

Backward uniformization computes recurrent count rewards, terminal risk and restricted mean survival. Cubic-Hermite interpolation evaluates actual-time jump and compensator terms of the martingale EIF. Each low-dimensional update changes every eligible intensity by a positive exponential factor, then recomputes the backward value and the EIF. Zero ineligible intensities remain zero. An empirical fold-stratum mixture is the common law for all targets. Conditional forecasts update its fold weights by the observed-history likelihood.

The fit retains individual EIF values, their means and variances, the estimator covariance, training subject indices, nuisance and targeted intensities, score trace, convergence flag, and an interpolation audit. A zero observed event mark is retained as an explicit boundary diagnostic, even if the numerical score is solved and the estimated EIF variance is positive. Gaussian inference is withheld at that boundary. A complete_followup=TRUE declaration specifies an uncensored design through the target horizon and is checked against the supplied histories; its censor hazard is zero. The automatic failure fallback uses observed lower and upper reward envelopes with Hoeffding-Bonferroni bounds, including censored histories; it does not require CAR. Uncapped nonnegative burdens receive [0, infinity]. Its support limits are prespecified scientific bounds, never the largest observed sample count. Complete follow-up makes the lower and upper observed rewards coincide; it is not inferred merely from observing no censoring in a sample.

The data-dependent automatic switch between Gaussian intervals and coarsening outer sets does not inherit an unconditional or selection-conditional finite-sample guarantee. Request interval="projection" throughout when invoking the unconditional coarsening-envelope theorem.

Initial nuisance cross-fitting does not by itself establish regularity of the validation-adaptive fluctuation. EIF efficiency and asymptotic inference require sufficient retained state, censoring conditional independence and positivity, nuisance product-rate conditions, gradient convergence, regular targeting, and negligible numerical approximation. The supplied implementation is a sufficient-state right-censoring model; it is not an unrestricted hidden-history or MNAR efficient estimator.
}
\value{The main fit is a coherentSurv_targeted object inheriting from coherentSurv. Its estimate vector has recurrent burdens, terminal risk and restricted mean survival for continuous targeting, or the declared feature means for finite-path targeting.}
\seealso{\code{cs_fit}, \code{cs_target}, \code{predict.coherentSurv_targeted}}
